---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/*
 * ariada.org/licenses — per-package SPDX license matrix and rationale.
 *
 * SPDX-License-Identifier: EUPL-1.2
 *
 * Public reference page documenting the SPDX (Software Package Data
 * Exchange) identifier on every published package, the rationale for
 * each choice (EUPL-1.2 / MIT / CC0-1.0 / CC-BY-4.0), the license-
 * compatibility surface, and the contribution-license posture (DCO,
 * not CLA).
 *
 * Source material: docs/PLATFORM_SPEC.md §5 + §11.2, master-strategy
 * license-choice memo research/output/2026-05-18-license-choice-eupl-
 * vs-alternatives.md §5-§6, PLATO v2.1 patch (TS-core OSS flip).
 *
 * Audience: NLnet evaluator, EU public-sector procurement reviewer,
 * downstream OSS contributor evaluating license obligations, REUSE-CLI
 * compliance auditor.
 *
 * Patent-disclosure geo-fencing:
 * The per-package license matrix section uses "patent-bound" /
 * "Patent A" / "Patent K" / "patent-blocked" wording in the per-row
 * rationale column — cumulative on a single page reinforces the
 * "patent-protected technology" reading test in UCPD (LANDAU audit
 * §2.4). That section is wrapped in a data-patent-disclosure="secondary"
 * element with a paired data-patent-fallback sibling. The CF Pages
 * Functions middleware at apps/ariada-org/functions/_middleware.ts
 * substitutes the fallback for visitors from non-allow-listed
 * jurisdictions. This is the canonical Strategy 2 wrap pattern from
 * .claude/skills/content-audit-legal/SKILL.md §5. Per audit 2026-05-20
 * (CRITICAL finding C-3), the earlier
 * `patent-audit-skip: no-patent-text-on-page` allow-list comment was
 * factually false and has been replaced with proper wrappers.
 */
---

<Base
  title="License matrix — ariada.org"
  description="Per-package SPDX license matrix for the @ariada-org/* family. EUPL-1.2 for compliance logic and scanner runtime, MIT for commodity tokens and embed badge, CC0-1.0 dual-license for the HTML test-fixture corpus. REUSE-compliant, DCO-based contribution."
>
  <section>
    <h1>License matrix</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The <code>@ariada-org/*</code> family ships under three OSS licenses
      and one documentation license. Every file in every published
      package carries an
      <abbr title="Software Package Data Exchange — Linux Foundation specification for license identifiers">SPDX</abbr>
      identifier in a header comment, and every package ships a
      <code>REUSE.toml</code> declaring per-file license and copyright.
      The repository is
      <a href="https://reuse.software/" rel="external">REUSE-compliant</a>;
      the REUSE CLI runs in continuous integration on every push so
      downstream auditors can verify license obligations without
      cloning.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section
    aria-labelledby="matrix-heading"
    data-patent-disclosure="secondary"
    data-patent-fallback="<h2 id='matrix-heading'>Per-package license matrix</h2><p>The per-package license matrix and per-row rationale (which references the OSS / HYBRID / patent-bound boundary per package) is geo-restricted on this surface for jurisdictions where the maintainer has not yet completed false-marking compliance review (DE / JP / FR / CN / KR / IN). OSS users receive patent peace for the published OSS surface under the EUPL-1.2 Article 2 grant; the full per-axis license matrix is available on request from EU/EEA residents via <a href='mailto:legal@ariada.org'>legal@ariada.org</a>. Per-file SPDX identifiers and REUSE.toml declarations remain visible in the <a href='https://github.com/ariada-org/ariada'>ariada-org/ariada</a> public monorepo for downstream license auditing.</p>"
  >
    <h2 id="matrix-heading">Per-package license matrix</h2>
    <p>
      Sixteen MUST-OSS packages: thirteen EUPL-1.2, three MIT, one
      dual EUPL-1.2 + CC0-1.0. Six HYBRID packages ship their
      commodity-outer surface under EUPL-1.2 (with the patented
      algorithmic core retained as separate proprietary code).
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Package</th>
            <th scope="col">License</th>
            <th scope="col">Files covered</th>
            <th scope="col">Rationale</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>wcag-rules-extended</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>All TS sources + JSON rule corpora</td>
            <td>Patent-bound rule corpus &mdash; narrow Article 2 grant + EU public-sector procurement compatibility</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>eaa-pipeline</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>Reusable workflow YAML + action sources</td>
            <td>Module B commodity-outer surface; Article 2 grant attaches to OSS workflow</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>statement-generator</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>All TS sources + locale bundles</td>
            <td>EN 301 549 art. 7 compliance logic &mdash; EU regulatory primacy</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>penalty-estimator</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2 (code) + CC-BY-4.0 (rate-card data)</td>
            <td>TS code + YAML rate cards</td>
            <td>EU-jurisdictional fine data &mdash; CC-BY-4.0 maximises downstream reuse for compliance research</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>evidence-emitter</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>All TS sources + JSON schemas</td>
            <td>VPAT + EN 301 549 evidence-bundle logic &mdash; EU-procurement-friendly</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>core-engine</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>TypeScript scanner orchestration core</td>
            <td>Non-patent-bound commodity scanner adapter; OSS by distribution model (npm-distributed TS = readable JS on install)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>core-browser</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>DOM adapter for Chrome / Edge / Firefox extension</td>
            <td>Commodity DOM adapter; Microsoft Insights ships the equivalent surface under MIT</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>core-playwright</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>Node + Chrome DevTools Protocol adapter</td>
            <td>Commodity orchestration glue; not patent-bound</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>cli</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>Command-line runner</td>
            <td>Wraps the OSS scanner runtime; gap-closing vs Pa11y</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>design-plugin-scaffolds</code></td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2</td>
            <td>Figma / UXP / Sketch plugin scaffolds</td>
            <td>Design-tool integration scaffolds; closed colour-suggestion engine stays separate under Patent A</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>L0-mindset-framework</td>
            <td>EUPL-1.2 (code) + CC-BY-4.0 (prose)</td>
            <td>Companion repo <code>ariada-org/l0-mindset-framework</code></td>
            <td>10-rule architect-tier framework; CC-BY-4.0 standard for educational prose</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Anti-overlay explainer</td>
            <td>CC-BY-4.0</td>
            <td>Documentation under <code>/anti-overlay/</code></td>
            <td>Public-interest regulator-aligned explainer; CC-BY-4.0 for translation + republication</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>brand-tokens</code></td>
            <td>MIT</td>
            <td>CSS custom-property definitions only</td>
